winebus: the field Sony's library claims on every packet, and we never have.

There is one byte-level difference left between what libScePad sends a
DualSense and what this driver sends it, and it is not the motors and not the
path. It is bit 0x40 of the second flag byte, and the byte that bit governs.

WHAT WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN HERE. mgvf-0007's header, from the disassembly of
libScePad 1.0.4.1 at image base 0x180000000: the library sets that bit in its
own send path at 0x1800072e2, "on every DualSense output report it sends",
together with report byte 37 -- written at 0x18000737b -- and bit 0x04 of report
byte 39. That last one is flag2's haptic bit, the one mgvf-0021 is about, so
the three are set together and mgvf-0007 called them "vibration fields". It
then let all of the second flag byte through untouched, because "nothing on
this disk says what the other bits of that byte mean". That was the right call
for a patch about audio. It is the wrong place to leave it for a patch about
how hard the pad shakes.

AND WHAT THE CAPTURE SAYS, hid-175158.log, a Bluetooth session in which a title
drives the pad through that library:

  native 0x31 writes                                    10,886
  with flag1 0x40                                       10,862
  with common byte 36 = 0x00                            10,860
  carrying a motor                                         283
  of those, with flag1 0x40 AND common 36 = 0x00            282

The last row is the one that matters. Every packet that title sent with a motor
in it claimed this field and wrote zero into it. The 283rd is a dump the trace
truncated, not a packet that did something else.

WHAT THE FIELD IS. The community's map reads common byte 36 as two nibbles of
power REDUCTION -- trigger motors in the low, rumble motors in the high, zero
meaning no reduction -- which would make that column of zeroes a request for
full power, repeated sixty times a second. That reading is consistent with the
disassembly, with the capture, and with the bit being set alongside the two
vibration fields rather than the audio ones. IT IS NOT PROVED BY ANY OF THEM.

SO THE OPTION CARRIES THE BYTE, NOT A YES OR NO. XInputRumbleMotorPower absent,
or 256 and up, leaves the field unclaimed: flag1 and common byte 36 go out
exactly as they came in, which is what this driver has always done and what the
host test asserts first. A value of 0 to 255 sets the bit and writes that byte.
0 is what libScePad sends. If the reading above is right then a large value
must be felt as WEAKER, and that is a test anybody can run with their hand and
two registry writes, which is worth more than a wiki.

WHY IT MIGHT MATTER AT ALL, said as a question and not as a finding. A field
nobody claims keeps whatever it was last set to. This driver has never written
it, so on a pad that some other application reduced -- or that powers on
reduced -- every measurement of "the haptic path feels weaker than the legacy
one" was made through an attenuator neither path knew about. That would be a
confound and not a conclusion, and one registry value now decides it.

BOTH WRITERS, as always. The ride stamps it into the game's own 0x31 and the
haptics thread puts it in its own packet, so the two cannot disagree about how
hard the pad should be allowed to shake -- except on a report too short to
reach common byte 36, where the thread simply does not claim it rather than
writing the byte somewhere else.

AND BOTH TRACE LINES NOW NAME THE TWO BYTES. mgvf-0016's says flag0 and
mgvf-0020's said only the motors, which was enough while those were the only
things either writer could get wrong. A field that goes out with the wrong
value and a field that never goes out at all read exactly alike in a log that
does not print it, and this patch is nothing but that field, so the ride's line
now carries flag0, flag1, flag2 and the power byte as the packet leaves. It is
the only place the ride's bytes can be seen at all: the client's own write was
dumped before we touched it and nothing dumps it again.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
@@ -151,6 +151,40 @@
 #define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY 0
 #define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC 1
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0023: the motor power field, and the bit that says the packet
+ * means it.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS ESTABLISHED, from the disassembly already written down in
+ * mgvf-0007's header: libScePad sets bit 0x40 of the SECOND flag byte on every
+ * DualSense output report it sends, in its own send path at 0x1800072e2,
+ * together with report byte 37 -- common byte 36 -- written at 0x18000737b,
+ * and bit 0x04 of report byte 39, which is flag2's haptic bit. mgvf-0007 named
+ * those three "vibration fields" and let them through untouched because
+ * nothing on this disk said what they meant.
+ *
+ * AND FROM THE CAPTURE, hid-175158.log, a Bluetooth session of a title driving
+ * the pad through that library: of 10,886 native 0x31 writes, 10,862 carry
+ * flag1 0x40 and 10,860 carry common byte 36 = 0x00; of the 283 that carry a
+ * motor, 282 carry both and the last is a truncated dump. So the library
+ * claims this field on every packet and writes zero into it.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED is the field's own arithmetic. The community's map
+ * reads it as two nibbles of power REDUCTION -- trigger motors low, rumble
+ * motors high, zero meaning none -- which would make libScePad's zero a
+ * request for full power on every packet. That reading is consistent with
+ * everything above and it is not proved by anything here.
+ *
+ * WHICH IS WHY THE OPTION CARRIES THE BYTE rather than a yes or no. Absent,
+ * or anything from 256 up, leaves the field alone: the packet does not claim
+ * it and the pad keeps whatever it was last told, which is what this driver
+ * has always done. A value of 0 to 255 sets the bit and writes that byte --
+ * 0 being exactly what libScePad sends. If the reading above is right, a
+ * non-zero value should be FELT AS WEAKER, and that is the test which decides
+ * it without anybody having to trust a wiki. */
+#define DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_POWER    36
+#define DUALSENSE_FLAG1_MOTOR_POWER     0x40
+#define DUALSENSE_MOTOR_POWER_UNCLAIMED 0x100
+
 /* MGVF mgvf-0016: the shortest output report that carries the whole of what
  * the rewrite reads. flag2 is the last of the four bytes and it is common byte
  * 38, so a USB report has to reach index 39 and therefore be 40 bytes long.
@@ -329,7 +363,8 @@
 int dualsense_bt_output_is_inert(const unsigned char *report, unsigned int len);
 unsigned int dualsense_bt_stamp_motors(unsigned char *report, unsigned int len,
                                        unsigned char right, unsigned char left,
-                                       unsigned int path, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32);
+                                       unsigned int path, unsigned int power,
+                                       dualsense_crc32_fn crc32);
 
 /* In place. Each returns len when the report was changed and 0 when it was
  * left as it was: too short to carry a CRC tail at all, or, for the read, the
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
@@ -570,7 +570,8 @@
 
 unsigned int dualsense_bt_stamp_motors(unsigned char *report, unsigned int len,
                                        unsigned char right, unsigned char left,
-                                       unsigned int path, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32)
+                                       unsigned int path, unsigned int power,
+                                       dualsense_crc32_fn crc32)
 {
     static const unsigned char seed = DUALSENSE_CRC_SEED_OUTPUT;
     unsigned char *common = report + DUALSENSE_BT_COMMON;
@@ -587,6 +588,14 @@
         common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG0] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG0_LEGACY_VIBRATION;
     common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_RIGHT] = right;
     common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_LEFT] = left;
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0023: claim the motor power field, or leave it to whatever the
+     * pad was last told. A 0x31 is long enough to carry it by definition --
+     * the length was checked above -- so there is no short-report case here. */
+    if (power < DUALSENSE_MOTOR_POWER_UNCLAIMED)
+    {
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG1] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG1_MOTOR_POWER;
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_POWER] = (unsigned char)power;
+    }
 
     crc = crc32(0, &seed, 1);
     crc = crc32(crc, report, DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN - DUALSENSE_CRC_TAIL);
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_haptics.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_haptics.h
@@ -118,6 +118,13 @@
 #define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC 1
 #endif
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0023: mirrored for the same reason -- the sentinel that says the
+ * packet is not claiming the motor power field. dualsense_usb.h carries the
+ * field's own header and the evidence for it. */
+#ifndef DUALSENSE_MOTOR_POWER_UNCLAIMED
+#define DUALSENSE_MOTOR_POWER_UNCLAIMED 0x100
+#endif
+
 #define DUALSENSE_HAPTICS_BT_DEADBAND      4
 
 /* MGVF mgvf-0020: how long the client has to be silent before the haptics
@@ -211,7 +218,8 @@
